# GIT :- Git is a version control system. Its Tool

It tracks: code changes, project history, who changed what, previous versions

Think of Git like: “Save points for your code project”

# GITHUB :- GitHub is a cloud platform where Git repositories are stored online.

GitHub helps with: backup, collaboration, sharing code, team projects, deployment integration

PHASE 1 - BASICS :-
git init
add
commit
status
log

PHASE 2 - GITHUB :-
create repo
push
pull
clone

PHASE 3 - BRANCHING :-
branch
checkout
merge
conflicts

PHASE 4 - ADVANCED :-
stash
reset
revert
SSH
PR workflow

# GitHub Workflow (MOST COMMON) :-
Clone Repo
↓
Create Branch
↓
Write Code
↓
Commit
↓
Push Branch
↓
Create Pull Request
↓
Review
↓
Merge


# Git File Lifecycle (VERY IMPORTANT): A file moves through these stages :-
Working Directory (our local repo)
↓
Staging Area (source control)
↓
Repository (main - remote/Github repo)


# Typical Daily Workflow :-
Write code
↓
Make several changes
↓
git add .
↓
git commit -m "Meaningful update"
↓
git push
That’s how developers usually work.


Important                Terms
Repository (Repo)	  Project folder tracked by Git
Commit	              Snapshot/save point
Branch	              Separate version of code
Merge	              Combine branches
Clone	              Copy repo from GitHub
Push	              Upload code to GitHub
Fetch                 Fetch downloads only.
Pull	              Downloads latest code + Merge
Remote	              Online GitHub repo
Local Repo	          Repo on your computer


# Important Git Commands Summary :-
Command	               Purpose
git init	            Start Git
git status	            Check changes
git add .	            Stage files
git commit -m ""	    Save snapshot
git log	                History
git branch	            View branches
git checkout	        Switch branch
git merge	            Merge branches
git push	            Upload
git pull	            Download + Merge
git clone	            Copy repo
git stash	            Temporary save


# STEPS TO USE GIT :- 

1. Git Setup :-
git config --global user.name 'your name(globally it will used everywhere)'
git config --global user.email 'your email Id '


# COMMANDS :-

2. git init :-
This creates hidden .git folder, Now Git starts tracking project.

3. git status :- 
Check Status

4. git add .  :-
Add all Files to Staging

5. git commit -m "Initial commit" :-
Commit message should explain changes.

6. git log / git log --oneline :-
Check Commit History

7. git branch feature-login :-
Create New Branch

git branch
All Local branches

git branch -r
All Remote branches

git branch -a
Both Local and Remote branches

8. git checkout feature-login / modern-way - git switch feature-login
Switch Branch

9. git checkout -b feature-login :-
Create + Switch Together

10. git branch :-
See All Branches, Current branch has *

11. git merge feature-login :-
Now feature code added to main.

12. .gitignore :-
Some files should NOT go to GitHub.
Examples: node_modules, .env, build folders 

PUSH LOCAL PROJECT TO GITHUB :-
S-1 : We have to create Repo on GitHub
S-2 : Connect Local Repo :-
13. git remote add origin https://github.com/VK18-HRSH/localrepo.git

PUSH CODE : For First Time :-
14. git push -u origin main
LOCAL → GITHUB
Upload local commits to remote GitHub repo
Git remembers : local main ↔ GitHub main
After That :- git push

15. git clone repo-url :-
Clone Repository, Download repo from GitHub

16. git pull origin main :-
GITHUB → LOCAL
Download + merge remote changes into local repo

17. git stash :-
Temporarily save unfinished work.

# What Are -m, -M, -u This :-
These are called: flags / options, They modify command behavior.

1. -m :- means : message
git commit -m "message"

2. -u :- Meaning : Connect local branch with remote branch.
git push -u origin main

3. -M :- means : move/rename branch forcefully
git branch -M main ==> like Here: master → main

4. -v :- means : Shows verbose/view details.
git remote -v

5. -f :- means : Force push, Dangerous sometimes.
git push -f

6. -b :- means : Creates New Branch
git checkout -b feature-login

7. -d : use : for local branch deletion(IF MERGED)
git branch -d feature-login

8. -D : use : for local branch deletion(IF NOT MERGED MEANS FORCE DELETE)
git branch -D feature-login

# IF YOU EVER STUCK IN VIM EDITOR THEN SIMPLY DO THIS :
How To Exit :-
Simply press :-  q / ! / :qw / :q!

# There are TWO types of branch deletion :-
1. Delete Local Branch :-
git branch -d feature-login

IMPORTANT : -d works only if : ✅ branch already merged
Otherwise Git protects you.

If branch not merged:
Force Delete (Dangerous) :-
git branch -D feature-login
Capital -D = force delete.

2. Delete Remote Branch (GitHub) :-
To remove branch from GitHub:

git push origin --delete feature-login

This deletes : feature-login, from GitHub remote repo.